WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.979
A well-designed movie poster accomplishes
many things at once.

00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:07.150
It shares basic information, like the title,
cast, and release date.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.429
It also indicates the film's genre and hints
at the plot.

00:00:10.429 --> 00:00:14.510
But the best posters go one step further,
capturing the movie's mood and atmosphere,

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.010
and getting you excited enough to buy a ticket.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:23.050
A poorly-designed movie poster, however, does
only one thing: it makes fans very, very angry.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.770
There may be no such thing as bad publicity,
but when promoting a movie, you want to make

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:28.750
fans excited, not annoyed.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.700
The following posters demonstrate why.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.270
Avatar

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.710
For the most part, this Avatar poster is fine.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.150
It's not the most exciting image, but it works,
at least until you get to the logo, and, more

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.790
specifically, the font.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:47.110
As far as typefaces go, Papyrus isn't quite
as hated as Comic Sans, but it's close.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:51.579
It's kitschy, boring, and constantly used
in inappropriate contexts.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:52.820
Graphic designers hate it.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.770
The font's creator, Chris Costello, even admitted
to CBS News that Papyrus is probably "overused."

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.620
"And again if I could take this time to apologize
to my brother and sister graphic designers,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.640
I'm a graphic designer as well."

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.880
In the years since, people haven't forgotten
about this misstep.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.330
In 2017, eight years after Avatar hit theaters,
Saturday Night Live aired a sketch skewering

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.330
the logo.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.140
"Do you remember the Avatar logo?"

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.320
"Uh, ya.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.780
It was tribal yet futuristic."

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:23.780
"Papyrus!"

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.360
Some things are simply unforgivable.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.650
Papyrus is one of them.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.210
The Heat

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:34.120
Look at the British poster for The Heat and,
without looking at the credits, see if you

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.140
can figure out who the two stars are.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.979
On the left, that's clearly Sandra Bullock
rocking the shades and tactical vest.

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:43.010
But who's that skinny, unrecognizable woman
on the right?

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.010
Surprise!

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.720
It's none other than Melissa McCarthy.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.659
She just looks a little different, thanks
to some Photoshop-assisted weight loss.

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:54.220
Naturally, McCarthy's fanbase didn't react
well to the poster's implicit body-shaming.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.130
The actress, however, handled the controversy
with her trademark sense of humor, jokingly

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.460
blaming her husband, telling The Hollywood
Reporter,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.750
"We had a big fight about it because I said
I wanted it 80 percent smaller and he said

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.439
he wanted it 70 percent."

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:09.379
Star Wars: The Force Awakens

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:13.389
The Force Awakens' official American poster
is exactly what you'd expect.

00:02:13.389 --> 00:02:17.879
Like previous Star Wars posters, it features
a painterly collage highlighting most of the

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:18.879
main cast.

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:23.569
Lead characters Rey and Finn get a big spotlight,
while main villain Kylo Ren lurks ominously

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:24.810
in the background.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:29.150
Han Solo gets a plum spot, as he deserves,
and some spaceships and stormtroopers round

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:30.290
the whole thing out.

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:35.379
The Chinese version is almost exactly the
same, except for one key difference: instead

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:39.040
of being front and center, Finn is absolutely
tiny.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.300
As far as anyone can tell, the only reason
Finn was shrunk is because John Boyega, the

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.760
actor who plays him, is black.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.959
At least that's what fans argued on Twitter,
and they seem to have a point: Poe Dameron

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:53.180
and Maz Kanata, who are also both played by
actors of color, were cut from the poster

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.180
entirely.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.499
The Force Awakens might take place a long
time ago in a galaxy far, far away, but racism

00:02:58.499 --> 00:03:01.299
doesn't have any place in the Star Wars universe.

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:04.040
X-Men: Apocalypse

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.279
Ask any X-Men fan, and they'll agree that
Apocalypse is a very bad guy.

00:03:08.279 --> 00:03:11.650
Still, there are ways to play up your villain's
villainy without resorting to cheap shock

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.650
tactics.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.129
In a heavily criticized X-Men Apocalypse poster,
Apocalypse's massive hand is wrapped around

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:18.420
Mystique's neck.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:23.319
It's a disturbing choice for a PG-13 superhero
flick, and people noticed the specific violence

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:25.359
towards Jennifer Lawrence's character.

00:03:25.359 --> 00:03:28.319
Actress Rose McGowan led the charge, telling
The Hollywood Reporter,

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:33.069
"There is a major problem when the men and
women at 20th Century Fox think casual violence

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:35.249
against women is the way to market a film."

00:03:35.249 --> 00:03:40.769
In response to the outcry, Fox quickly apologized
and removed the poster from circulation.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:42.389
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:45.299
Spider-Man: Homecoming's marketing got off
to a great start.

00:03:45.299 --> 00:03:49.590
First, Tom Holland's Peter Parker made his
debut in Captain America: Civil War and almost

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.060
stole the entire movie.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.959
Next, there were some well-received teaser
posters, which let everyone know that this

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:59.579
was the laid back, fun, and fully MCU-integrated
webslinger we'd been clamoring to see for

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:00.579
years.

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:04.540
But then the final poster arrived, and to
call it a mess would be kind.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.699
Characters appear both in and out of costume
for no reason, and nothing flows, making the

00:04:08.699 --> 00:04:11.739
final product look like a rushed Photoshop
job.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:16.440
Why Sony decided that this muddled, busy image
should represent a highly anticipated Spider-Man

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.320
flick remains a mystery, especially considering
that some of the alternate options are pretty

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.320
darn good.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.420
"Cannot take this anymore, I gotta, I gotta
get outa here."

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.120
mother!

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:33.370
Audiences either love, hate, or love to hate
Darren Aronofsky's mother!, a bizarre psychological

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:38.210
horror-drama about a married couple, their
unwelcome house guests, and an emerging cult.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:42.180
Shortly after release, Paramount doubled down
on the film's divisive reception with a brand

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.540
new poster that championed mother! as "the
most controversial movie in decades."

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.540
Naturally, that poster was also controversial,
and again, Jennifer Lawrence was the victim

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.730
of violence.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:55.980
The main image splits Lawrence's face in two,
with one side beaten and battered.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.030
Even people who liked the movie took issue
with the new poster.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:00.200
Critic Esther Zuckerman tweeted,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.730
"I don't care what you think of the movie,
the fact that a woman gets beat up in it shouldn't

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:05.040
be the selling point."

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.250
Paramount representatives have declined to
comment on the poster's divided reaction,

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.640
although they have publicly stuck up for the
film itself.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.400
"You scared me"

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:19.780
Tomb Raider

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:23.840
Even if you don't play video games, you probably
know Lara Croft and her infamous assets.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.340
But in 2013, the Tomb Raider video games series
reboot toned down Lara's exaggerated physical

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.800
attributes and ramped up her ferocious determination
and peerless bow-and-arrow skills.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.660
For the 2018 film adaptation, Alicia Vikander
proved to be a strong fit for this version

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:39.850
of the character.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:42.890
This is a Lara Croft who's cunning, smart,
and tough.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.470
While she's certainly not hard on the eyes,
there's much more to her than her looks.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.580
But you wouldn't know that from the poster,
in which some poorly done photo manipulation

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:55.090
unnaturally elongates Vikander's neck, inviting
comparisons to a velociraptor.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:58.900
Ultimately, this wasn't a problem in the film
itself, but in the lead-up to the release,

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.910
it was hard to think about anything else.

